		{
			"title": "Which fizz?",
			"text": "Watch: magnesium fizzes hard and is soon gone; zinc fizzes slowly, iron more slowly still; copper does nothing. Wait for the magnesium to be gone.",
			"why": "A metal more reactive than hydrogen gives its electrons to the acid's hydrogen ions: Mg + 2 H+ -> Mg2+ + H2. Magnesium's own hydrogen stirs the acid round it, so it goes as fast as the acid reaches it; zinc and iron are held back at their surfaces, where hydrogen forms only slowly; copper is less reactive than hydrogen.",
			"checks": [
